  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>A small wooded canyon with a stream running through it, lined with impressive 80-metre-high red cliffs — a striking, grand landscape that brings Corsica to mind, in the Pays de Fayence. The walk takes about 2h30 and isn't difficult, with shaded undergrowth.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>An important caveat: the Pays de Fayence tourist office states that there is no water in the gorges in summer, even though the coolness of the undergrowth is very much there — so it's not a swimming spot, the site being better known for climbing and the Grotte du Muéron. The gorges are in Bagnols-en-Forêt; the hike's waymarking is hard to see in places. In the neighbouring Roquebrune-sur-Argens massif, access to the massifs is regulated from 15 June to 15 September: check the day's map in summer. The tourist office publishes no particular dog rule for this site: keep yours close, especially around the cliffs where climbers are at work. The view from the heights takes in the Estérel massif and the Roquebrune rock — a walk that's mainly worth it for the view rather than a guaranteed swim.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: pair this outing with the nearby Gorges de la Siagne and de la Siagnole for a full day in the area.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The hilltop villages of Fayence, Callian or Montauroux are charming for extending the day.</p>
